{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Prototype code is disposable and its only job is to help the team learn quickly, so accepting AI-written code without reading it is reasonable. Keep the experiment isolated from real credentials, customer data, and production systems."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A stakeholder or investor demo has reputational stakes even though the code is still disposable, so selectively review risky paths and test the demo flow end to end. Full vibe coding is too fragile when a live failure could undermine the pitch."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "Early users create real security, privacy, and reliability exposure, but the product still needs speed and may be substantially rewritten. Review high-risk changes line by line and require tests that exercise behavior, especially around auth, payments, and data handling."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and an ongoing maintenance burden make every change part of the team's long-term liability. AI may write the code, but a person should review each change line by line, with tests and ownership rather than behavior checks alone."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "Mature, regulated, and safety-critical systems require deep human understanding, accountability, and auditable authorship. Use AI to suggest approaches, tests, and alternatives, but have a person write or rewrite every line and apply the required formal verification and independent review."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Treat these as default maximum levels rather than a single global setting: 4 for disposable exploration, 3 for demos and early MVPs, 2 for production, and 1 for critical systems. Any level can be tightened for higher-risk work, and no level permits exposing secrets or real customer data to AI tools without explicit approval."
}

